<ShortAnswer>
  open a new tab and click Customize Chrome at the bottom right to change its background, colours
  and shortcuts. Chrome has no setting for putting a page of your own there; for that you need an
  extension that replaces the new tab page, such as TabCabin.
</ShortAnswer>

<h2>1. Customize Chrome's own new tab page</h2>
<p>The page with the Google search box can change its look, not what it holds.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open a new tab and click Customize Chrome at the bottom right. A side panel opens.</li>
  <li>
    Change the theme: pick a background from the gallery or upload a picture of your own. Chrome
    suggests colours to match it.
  </li>
  <li>
    Under Shortcuts, turn them off, or choose between the sites you visit most and shortcuts you add
    yourself.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  Good for a calmer page in a minute. It is still the same search box and a row of shortcuts, with
  no room for notes or anything longer.
</p>

<h2>2. Open your own page with the Home button or at startup</h2>
<p>Two settings come close, although neither changes a new tab.</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    In Settings → Appearance, turn on Show Home button and enter a web address. The Home button next
    to the address bar now opens that page.
  </li>
  <li>
    In Settings → On startup, choose Open a specific page or set of pages, and Chrome opens them
    every time it starts.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  Good when one website is where you start the day. New tabs still open Chrome's own page, because
  Chrome has no setting for a new tab address.
</p>

<h2>3. Replace the new tab page with an extension</h2>
<p>
  Extensions are the only way to put something else in every new tab: a dashboard, a quote, a to-do
  list or your saved tabs. A few things are worth knowing first.
</p>
<ul class="mt-4 list-disc pl-6">
  <li>Only one extension at a time can own the new tab page.</li>
  <li>
    The first time a new tab opens, Chrome may ask whether this is the page you expected. Keep it to
    stay with the extension.
  </li>
  <li>
    To go back, open the ⋮ menu → Extensions → Manage Extensions and turn the extension off or
    remove it.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Boards in every new tab with TabCabin</h2>
<p>
  TabCabin is a free, open-source extension that turns the new tab page into boards of tabs, links,
  notes and to-dos. Instead of a search box, every new tab shows the things you are working on.
</p>
<ol>
  <li>Get TabCabin for Chrome, Edge or Brave and open a new tab.</li>
  <li>
    Pick how to start: a starter board with a few tips, an empty board, or one of the{' '}
    <a href="/templates/">ready-made templates</a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Drag open tabs from the panel on the left into groups, and add notes or to-dos next to them.
  </li>
  <li>
    In Settings → Appearance, choose a light or dark theme and a compact density if you want more on
    the screen. Under Settings → Data, Most visited sites → Add puts the sites Chrome's shortcuts
    used to show into a group.
  </li>
</ol>
